  "question": "How does the SARS-CoV-2 virus enter host cells and what are the validated therapeutic strategies targeting different stages of infection?",
  "llm_response": "SARS-CoV-2 enters host cells through the ACE2 receptor, with the spike protein binding to ACE2 and TMPRSS2 facilitating membrane fusion. Once inside, the virus hijacks cellular machinery for replication using RNA-dependent RNA polymerase (RdRp). Validated treatments include: Remdesivir inhibits RdRp to block viral replication; Paxlovid inhibits the main protease (Mpro) essential for viral polyprotein processing; for severe cases with hyperinflammation, Dexamethasone reduces mortality by suppressing excessive immune response via NF-κB pathway inhibition; Tocilizumab blocks IL-6 signaling to mitigate cytokine storm; and Baricitinib inhibits JAK-STAT signaling. These treatments target distinct phases of infection and immune dysregulation.",
